Tiered downsampling for single-node VictoriaMetrics OSS. ~210 line
source patch, not a fork. No cluster support.

  -downsampling.period=30d:5m,180d:1h

Last sample per 5m for data older than 30d, per 1h older than 180d.
Same semantics as -dedup.minScrapeInterval, no aggregation. Older tiers
need bigger intervals, divisible by newer ones. Queries thin
immediately, disk reclaimed by the hourly historical merge watcher.

Build and test a release (Go and Python 3 required):

  ./build.sh v1.155.0

The script clones a fresh upstream checkout, requires a strict patch application,
runs the complete storage and query-merge test suites, builds a static linux-amd64
binary, and tests query thinning and disk merges using an isolated database.
Artifacts and SHA256 checksums are written to dist/. Source is retained under
work/. An optional second argument reuses a clean checkout at the requested tag;
the script never resets an existing checkout.

Or apply manually from inside a pristine upstream checkout:

  git apply /path/to/vmdownsamplepatch/vm-downsampling.patch
  cp /path/to/vmdownsamplepatch/downsampling_test.go lib/storage/
  go test ./lib/storage ./app/vmselect/netstorage -count=1
  make victoria-metrics-prod

Validated 2026-11-03 against v1.155.0 (3fec2f72414d00d1acda807c8818d7af90cc966d).
The patch needed only refreshed upstream context; its tier logic is unchanged.
The runtime smoke test feeds 60 one-minute samples to each of three age tiers:
2 hourly samples, 13 five-minute samples, and all 60 recent samples survive.
It then restarts with tiers disabled and checks the same data, proving that
the merge reclaimed samples on disk rather than merely hiding them at query time.

  python3 smoke-test.py /path/to/victoria-metrics-prod

Set retention to cover the data, e.g.
-retentionPeriod=2y -downsampling.period=30d:5m,180d:1h

Downsampling is destructive. Back up before changing tiers or upgrading.
Monthly partitions are scheduled using their newest timestamp, so an entire
month must age beyond a tier boundary before its final thinning merge runs.
Zero scheduled partitions alone does not mean downsampling is broken.

Apache 2.0, same as upstream. Independent reimplementation, no
enterprise code. Unofficial, not affiliated with VictoriaMetrics, Inc.
